*** What is SCRAM ***

SCRAM is a size composition resolved aerosol model.
It can simulate coagulation, condensation/evaporation and nucleation processes
of externally mixed particles.

*** Compilation and Installation ***

First you should have a Linux system and
make sure the construction tool: SCONS has already been installed.
If not you can obtain it through the following instruction of the site below:
http://www.scons.org/wiki/SconsTutorial1

Then you can do the compilation by a simple commend:
scons

Required libraries :  netcdf

*** Coefficient Repartition ***

Two coefficient repartition data files are already provided under the
main path:
coef_s1_f1_b7.nc for internal mixing
coef_s5_f3_b7.nc for external mixing

If you want to compute those database by yourself, then you need
to compile and install an separate program under the folder:
COEF_REPATITION

*** Run ***

The program should be executed with a configuration file which
contains all the initial condition and parametrization information.

Two examples are provide under the folder:
INIT

For example, you can repeat the all dynamic external mixing
simulation within the article with following command:
./ProgramSCRAM INIT/cfg_megapole_01072009.cfg

*** Contact ***
If you have further questions, please contact:
zhus@cerea.enpc.fr

*** SCRAMBoxApp note: building and debugging this tree ***

This source tree is shared by both platforms, but each side uses it differently:

- Linux (automated, scheduled troubleshooting only): build and install the native core with
  `bash scripts/linux/build_runtime.sh` from the repository root. That wrapper runs scons with
  `FC=gfortran` and installs the binary into `core/executables_or_wrappers/runtime/linux/`.
  Scheduled rounds: `bash scripts/linux/auto_round.sh [quick|standard|deep]`. Toolchain in
  `scripts/linux/`, documents in `docs/linux_debugging/`.
- Windows (manual development and debugging, verification/review, release packaging): the shipped
  runtime is `runtime/windows/ProgramSCRAM.exe`; rebuilding it needs a Windows Fortran/C toolchain
  (see the README in `runtime/windows/`). Packaging is done with `scripts/package_app_windows.ps1`
  and `scripts/make_windows_devkit.ps1` — never from Linux.

Do not run bare `scons` for a release build: the wrappers carry the required flags and the
Windows/POSIX differences (`SRC/ModuleCoeffRepartitionBoxmodel.f90`, `coeff_make_dir`).
